      "lines": [
        {"id":"DEV-001","mr":"दुर्गे दुर्घट भारी तुजविण संसारी ।","ro":"Dur-ge Dur-ghat Bhaa-ree Tuj-vin San-saa-ree","type":"verse"},
        {"id":"DEV-002","mr":"अनाथनाथे अंबे करुणा विस्तारी ।","ro":"A-naath-naa-the Am-be Ka-ru-naa Vis-taa-ree","type":"verse"},
        {"id":"DEV-003","mr":"वारी वारी जन्ममरणाते वारी ।","ro":"Vaa-ree Vaa-ree Jan-ma-ma-ra-naa-te Vaa-ree","type":"verse"},
        {"id":"DEV-004","mr":"हारी पडलो आता संकट निवारी ॥१॥","ro":"Haa-ree Pa-da-lo Aa-taa San-kat Ni-vaa-ree","type":"verse"},
        {"id":"DEV-R01","mr":"जय देवी जय देवी महिषासुरमथिनी ।","ro":"Jai De-vee Jai De-vee Ma-hi-shaa-sur-ma-thi-nee","type":"refrain"},
        {"id":"DEV-R02","mr":"सुरवरईश्वरवरदे तारक संजीवनी ॥धृ॥","ro":"Sur-var-eesh-war-var-de Taa-rak San-jee-va-nee","type":"refrain"},
        {"id":"DEV-005","mr":"त्रिभुवनभुवनी पाहता तुजऐसी नाही ।","ro":"Tri-bhu-van-bhu-va-nee Paa-ha-taa Tuj-ai-see Naa-hee","type":"verse"},
        {"id":"DEV-006","mr":"चारी श्रमले परंतु न बोलवे काही ।","ro":"Chaa-ree Shra-ma-le Pa-ran-tu Na Bo-la-ve Kaa-hee","type":"verse"},
        {"id":"DEV-007","mr":"साही विवाद करिता पडिले प्रवाही ।","ro":"Saa-hee Vi-vaad Ka-ri-taa Pa-di-le Pra-vaa-hee","type":"verse"},
        {"id":"DEV-008","mr":"ते तू भक्तालागी पावसी लवलाही ॥२॥","ro":"Te Too Bhak-taa-laa-gee Paa-va-see La-va-laa-hee","type":"verse"},
        {"id":"DEV-009","mr":"प्रसन्नवदने प्रसन्न होसी निजदासा ।","ro":"Pra-sann-va-da-ne Pra-sann Ho-see Nij-daa-saa","type":"verse"},
        {"id":"DEV-010","mr":"क्लेशापासुनि सोडवी तोडी भवपाशा ।","ro":"Kle-shaa-paa-su-ni So-da-vee To-dee Bha-va-paa-shaa","type":"verse"},
        {"id":"DEV-011","mr":"अंबे तुजवाचून कोण पुरविल आशा ।","ro":"Am-be Tuj-vaa-choon Kon Pu-ra-vil Aa-shaa","type":"verse"},
        {"id":"DEV-012","mr":"नरहरि तल्लीन झाला पदपंकजलेशा ॥३॥","ro":"Na-ra-ha-ri Tal-leen Jhaa-laa Pad-pan-kaj-le-shaa","type":"verse"}
      ],
      "meaning": "The Mother is called by the name that means the un-crossable: Durga, without whom this world is too hard to cross. She is refuge for those who have none, and the devotee asks her plainly to end the round of birth and death and to lift the danger at hand. The middle verses confess that the four Vedas and the six schools of philosophy wore themselves out and still could not describe her, yet she comes at once to anyone who simply loves her. Narahari, the poet, ends lost at the dust of her lotus feet."
    },
    {
